 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * The "browser bridge" shield: the peripherals a guest gains when the QEMU
 * machine is qemu-wasm running in a browser tab. It is not a physical shield —
 * the devices are provided by patched QEMU models fed by page JavaScript (see
 * tools/qemu-patches/ and tools/qemu-jit-patches/).
 *
 * Everything lives in the per-machine files under boards/: the devices sit at
 * machine-specific addresses (an MMIO gap on the LM3S6965, a spare PL011 slot
 * on virt), so there is nothing meaningful to add for an arbitrary board.
 *
 * boards/qemu_cortex_m3.overlay — GNSS NMEA UART, qemu,host-sensor (with
 *   accel0 / temp0 / light0 / … aliases so stock sensor samples build against
 *   the host sensor), qemu,host-gpio, qemu,host-audio (I2S out), and
 *   qemu,host-mic (DMIC in, aliased dmic0).
 * boards/qemu_cortex_a53.overlay — GNSS NMEA UART, browser-sized qemu,ramfb,
 *   the same host-sensor node/aliases, qemu,host-audio, qemu,host-mic, and two
 *   virtio-mmio devices declared disabled behind snippets: virtio,gpu
 *   (-S virtio-gpu) and virtio,gpio (-S virtio-gpio).
 *
 * The two boards reach GPIO differently. The Cortex-M3 has qemu,host-gpio, a
 * bespoke register block: fast (a pin read never leaves the guest) but with no
 * interrupt line, so its gpio-keys is polled. The Cortex-A53 has a standard
 * VIRTIO GPIO device, whose driver is stock Zephyr and whose interrupts are
 * real, at the cost of a virtqueue round trip per operation. The LM3S6965 has
 * no virtio-mmio bus, which is why the M3 keeps the MMIO device rather than
 * both boards sharing one.
 */
